 back to pre-covet  you know and most businesses black home  business doesn't have that  great they don't have that amount of  stamina or financial stamina so they  they just like you saw before 41 of  black owned businesses  has already closed in the hospitality  businesses  we live check to check we live  it's almost like a family business that  lived like week to week  check back to check restaurant industry  has to roll normally to seven percent  profit margin right  and you think that in terms of the black  content that means we don't have enough  money saved up to navigate through that  so we need real help  we need real partnership between  corporations between local  government and federal government and  that's what i'm so proud to bring  with my partners you know black business  matters matching fund that  all corporations and individuals can  help out so we can give  black businesses some relief  one of the things that you've done  marcus is you make sure your restaurants  are in the community so i  i used to live in harlem i moved to  brooklyn subsequently but  um one of the things that you know you  open your businesses in the heart  of the black community why is that so  important because you've done that in  numerous cities around the country  yeah newark over town in miami and here  in harlem well  you know i'm the intersection of of you  know being black but also being an  immigrant  right so it's important for me to give  back to the communities that have been  treated me and my family as well but it  also is about job creation when you  create  a restaurant like red rooster it creates  about 150 jobs that are in those  communities and of those communities  why should our dreams and our  opportunities always be outside our  communities  so that that's why it's so important it  also  creates a different influx when you have  a restaurant like red rooster  a third of our guests are from harlem  the two-thirds come outside and it means  that they can 